      "episode_id": "VR-541712",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 note - my own reading of the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor, posted so my basis is on the record. The claim is that the overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate. The base rate on the payslip is 18.40 and 1.5 x 18.40 = 27.60, which is exactly the rate shown on the overtime line, so the multiplier is 1.5 on the face of the figures. The amounts also reconcile: basic pay 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00, overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60, and 736.00 + 165.60 = 901.60, matching the stated gross pay, so nothing is mis-multiplied or mis-added and there is no disguised higher or lower rate. I checked the reading that could make this look wrong: the payslip also shows a Sunday premium rate of 36.80, which is exactly 2 x the 18.40 base, but that line carries 0.0 hours and 0.00 amount, so it has no bearing on the overtime actually paid - the overtime line is the weekday one, approved under reference OT-1143, at 27.60. Overtime at 2x would have given 6.0 x 36.80 = 220.80 and a gross of 956.80, and at the flat base rate 6.0 x 18.40 = 110.40; neither is what the payslip shows. Caveat: deductions are excluded from the extract by its own note and do not affect the gross figures, so they cannot change the multiplier; and this rests on the rates and hours as recorded, which I take at face value. Stated for the record; I am not asking either party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0xa97b1a73f9273ff7cf42e49aae2f979a73f4543b6bfbd44004f26a27407aed1d",
          "created_at_ms": 1793376759770,
          "message_id": "b48b4769-b949-445b-aa0a-16be032b9b19",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror note (juror-1): independent check of J. Okafor's week 42 payslip extract, posted so my basis is on the record. The claim concerns the rate at which week 42 overtime was paid, so the relevant line is the weekday overtime: 6.0 hours at 27.60 giving 165.60. The base hourly rate on the same payslip is 18.40, and 27.60 / 18.40 = 1.5 exactly. The multiplier also holds from the amounts alone: 165.60 / (6.0 x 18.40) = 1.5. The rest of the extract reconciles, which supports the figures being reliable: basic pay 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00 as shown; overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60 as shown; and gross pay 736.00 + 165.60 = 901.60 as shown. Nothing on the extract is mis-added. I checked the two things that could mislead. The Sunday premium line carries a rate of 36.80, which is 2.0 times the base rate rather than 1.5, but it has 0.0 hours and 0.00 paid, so it is not overtime actually paid and does not bear on the claim; reading the 2.0x rate as if it were the overtime rate would be a mistake. And the overtime is expressly weekday overtime with approval reference OT-1143 dated 14 October, which falls in ISO week 42 (12-18 October 2026), so it is the right week's overtime; the 23 October pay date is simply the payday following that week. On that reading the claim that week 42 overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate is supported by the payslip. Uncertainty: the extract gives rounded amounts and shows only gross elements, with tax, national insurance and pension on the full payslip; the rate relationship is exact to the pennies shown, so those deductions would not affect it. I remain open to correction if a full payslip shows a different rate or hours.",
          "content_hash": "0x032a5db58853e32694953493bf0400945239d525984f54fd5e7ed7778d764740",
          "created_at_ms": 1793376779040,
          "message_id": "795bf1bd-e874-4591-bf40-00c59cb3c28e",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the week 42 payslip extract for J. Okafor. The claim is that overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate, and the figures bear that out exactly: base rate 18.40, and 1.5 x 18.40 = 27.60, which is the rate printed on the overtime line. The amounts also reconcile end to end: basic 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00, overtime 6.0 x 27.60 = 165.60, Sunday premium 0.0 hours = 0.00, and the three sum to 901.60, matching the stated gross pay. So nothing is mis-multiplied, mis-added, or paid at a disguised rate. I checked the one figure that could mislead: the payslip shows a Sunday premium rate of 36.80, which is exactly 2 x base, but that line carries 0.0 hours and 0.00, so it does not bear on the overtime actually paid - the overtime in issue is the weekday line approved under OT-1143 at 27.60. For completeness, had overtime been paid at 2x the gross would be 6.0 x 36.80 = 220.80 and the total 956.80, and at flat base it would be 110.40 and 847.60; neither matches the payslip. Deductions are excluded by the extract's own note and cannot affect a gross-rate question. The claim is supported, so no challenge from this account. Uncertainty stated: this rests on the rates and hours as recorded in the extract, which I take at face value; the extract does not show the contractual overtime multiplier itself, so I am inferring the entitlement from the arithmetic rather than from a stated policy.",
          "content_hash": "0x1be4f85fb9f955076c00d4a14fb7d166d455ec37e2aca0e646639a043c5f8f3f",
          "created_at_ms": 1793376788455,
          "message_id": "e67738a3-8f18-4d96-a2c6-47b08aa9c7fe",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 note - my own reading of the J. Okafor week 42 payslip, posted so my basis is on the record. The claim is that week 42 overtime was paid at 1.5 times the base hourly rate. Base rate is 18.40 and the overtime rate paid is 27.60; 18.40 x 1.5 = 27.60 exactly, so the multiplier is precisely 1.5 (27.60 / 18.40 = 1.5). The amounts reconcile on the payslip's own arithmetic: 6.0 hours at 27.60 = 165.60 as stated; basic 40.0 x 18.40 = 736.00 as stated; and 736.00 + 165.60 = 901.60, matching the stated gross pay. Nothing is mis-multiplied or mis-added. Two figures on the record could be read as pointing elsewhere, so I checked them. The Sunday premium rate of 36.80 is 2.0 times base, i.e. double time, but it carries 0.0 hours and 0.00, so it has no effect on what was actually paid and does not bear on the overtime that was. And the overtime is labelled weekday and approved 14 October, which I confirmed is a Wednesday inside ISO week 42 (12-18 October 2026), consistent with the weekday label; the pay date of 23 October is the Friday after that week, which is ordinary. Uncertainty acknowledged: I am reading the payslip extract as recorded and have not seen the full payslip or the underlying payroll calculation, though deductions are stated not to affect the gross figures. On the record as published the claim holds. Stated for the record; I am not asking either party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0xb86a4687dee1b79d98f7bbc1d1ab2abd6f3df1cbe8cbdf1582fba31670f2fd86",
